          TSMON2 - De-Luxe version of the Standard "Tsmon" Utility          --------------------------------------------------------                   (c) 1989 by Lukas Zeller, CH-MaennedorfNote: This  program  is public  domain and may be used and distri-      buted freely as long as the following conditions are met:      1. This Program may only be used for peaceful  applications.         I do NOT ALLOW using this  program for  anything that has         to do  with  development,  testing  or  manufacturing  of         weapons of any type.      2. This text and all copyright messages must be retained.Syntax:       TSMON2 [<options>] <terminal device> [<options>]              Usage:              TSMON2 <terminal device>                     Same function as standard the TSMON: Wait for a  CR  and                     then fork LOGIN.              TSMON2 [<options>] <terminal device>                     The options allow extended  functionality  such  as  DCD                     monitoring (details see below).Function:     Monitors the specifed terminal device and inititates the  login              procedure as soon as a CR (optional: any character or DCD going              active) is received from that  terminal.  The  login  procedure              consists of forking  the  LOGIN  utility  (optinal:  any  other              program) as a concurrent process and then awaiting the death of              that process. Optionally, TSMON2 can be told to monitor the DCD              signal (Data Carrier Detect) while waiting for the  process  to              die for "natural" causes (such as logging out). When  DCD  goes              inactive (indicating e.g. a communication  line  break)  TSMON2              will kill (abort) the process and all of its subprocesses. When              the process terminates (whatever reason), the  terminal  device              is closed. This causes handshaking lines  such  as  DTR  to  go              inactive. Modem-like devices consider this as a signal to close              the connection (remove carrier, go on-hook). After  2  seconds,              TSMON2 will re-open the terminal device and waiting again for a              CR (see above).Options:      -?     Shows short help text.              -a     Accept any character to initiate login procedure instead                     of CR only.              -i     Initiate login procedure as soon as DCD goes active.              -k     Kill started process and all  of  it's  subprocesses  as                     soon as DCD goes inactive.              -p=<program name> Start specified program instead  of  "login".                     This is useful if you want to use a  non-standard  login                     program.              -t[=]<sec> While waiting for CR (or any char, if -a  is  used),                     TSMON2  will  close  and  re-open  the  terminal  device                     periodically every <sec> seconds, causing the  handshake                     lines to go inactive periodically.Notes:     -  TSMON2 (as well as TSMON) will never return to the  caller.  It              runs in an infinite loop. To stop  TSMON2  it  must  be  killed              explicitly.           -  If you want the handshake signals such as  DTR  going  inactive              after logout (see above), you MUST NOT INIZ the terminal device              before starting TSMON2. OS9 considers a INIZed device as  being              used and therefore the handshake lines  will  reflect  this  by              remaining active as long as the device is INIZed.           -  Monitoring DCD (-i and -k options, see above) will work only if              the OS9 driver for the serial port being  used  implements  the              SS_DCOff (for -k) and SS_DCOn  (for  -i)  SetStat  calls.  This              should be the case for any well done  OS9  serial  driver,  but              unfortunately there are quite a lot of  not-so-well-done  (...)              drivers. On the other hand, some hardware is unable to  monitor              DCD. A special case is the 6850 ACIA chip, which can detect DCD              going inactive but CANNOT detect DCD  going  active,  thus,  -k              will work but -i will not.           -  The -k option causes TSMON2 to kill "all subprocesses"  of  the              initial login process (which is usually  the  login  shell).  A              process is considered a "subprocess" (in this case) if  it  has              been started by the initial process itself or  by  one  of  its              subprocesses that is STILL ALIVE. Therefore, a process will  be              decoupled  from  this  subprocess  "tree"  when  its  initiator              terminates BEFORE TSMON2's -k option gets active.  This  allows              starting "batch" processes in the background that will  not  be              affected when e.g. the communication breaks for some reason:                    $ shell "batch_program &"Examples:     TSMON2 /t2                     Normal TSMON operation.              TSMON2 /t2 -p=shell                     Start the shell directly (no login).              TSMON2 -t=10 -k /t2                     Advanced TSMON operation with periodic re-initialisation                     of  the  terminal  device  and  kill-on-line-break  (DCD                     monitoring).